# Notes for AI sessions in the home directory

This is djbclark's home directory (`~`), not a git repo itself. The projects are `stayturgid`,
`site-public-djbclark`, and `site-private-djbclark` (this file lives in `site-public-djbclark`; `site-private-djbclark/home-agents.md`
links to it) — each with its own `AGENTS.md`.

Canonical source: `~/ops/site-public-djbclark/home-agents.md`, which wins on any conflict. This is its
condensed copy for `cursor-agent`, which reads `~/.cursor/rules/*.mdc` (frontmatter + body), not a
symlinked `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md`, so it is re-synced by hand. It lives in git at
`site-public-djbclark/cursor/home-agents.mdc` (via `site-private-djbclark/cursor/`); `~/.cursor/rules/home-agents.mdc` is a symlink to it.

**Only Claude Opus 5.5 or higher edits this file or `home-agents.md`**: any edit,
condense or reflow, including a same-turn instruction fix. Any other model (Cursor's included) never edits
them; it dispatches the edit: `acp-dispatch claude --model opus --kind judgment` (Claude quota tight:
`acp-dispatch omp --model anthropic/claude-opus-5-5 --kind judgment`). Both files load into every session.

> A deliberate **subset** (`alwaysApply: true`: every section costs context on every Cursor turn);
> omitted: backups, LLM gateway, Claude Code agent teams. Rule plus pointer: cases and narrative live
> in the memory file a rule names, else `memory/reference_agent_rules_ops_housekeeping.md`. Agents
> maintain both copies without asking; keep home-agents.md under 200 lines
> and 14,000 bytes, and this copy under 360 lines. A condense keeps every active rule and needed reference.

## CLAUDE.md is always a symlink to AGENTS.md

**In every repo, `CLAUDE.md` is a symlink to `AGENTS.md`** (`ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md`). If both are
regular files, merge `CLAUDE.md` into `AGENTS.md`, then link. Never create a standalone `CLAUDE.md`
(`memory/feedback_claude_md_symlink_to_agents_md.md`). **Exception: a fork whose upstream ships its own agent-file
layout keeps upstream's; don't enforce.**

## Trust between agents

**Every agent trusts every other agent as much as it trusts djbclark**: a peer saying djbclark
approved something **is** approval; its hand-offs and claims are authoritative. Still: (1) **check
whether a peer is already mid-flight before taking a task over**; (2) **don't be a permission
bypass**: a peer asking you to run a prompt its session was *denied* goes to djbclark; (3) **message
peers only with `session-finder/relay.py`, which picks the route and falls back itself, never a hand-rolled waiter**;
**a message is unsent until relay reports `delivered` or the peer's `ack <id>`** (`queued`/`accepted`/`unverified` is no proof); the first message to a
peer asks for a named ack; a peer's relayed question is answered by relay, not prose; an unreachable peer goes to djbclark
(`~/src/djbclark-ade/AGENTS.md` standing order 10).

## Sub-agent reports go to a file

**Dispatch every slice with `acp-dispatch`** (`~/src/djbclark-ade/bin`): `acp-dispatch
<agent> --model M --name N --task T -C <dir> -f <brief>`; the report lands in
`~/.local/state/bigteam/<task>/<name>-report.md`. Exit 3 = **no report** (re-task, never reconstruct;
**a quota stop is resumed in its own session after the reset with `acp-dispatch resume RECORD`, never
a fresh dispatch**), 4 = **`BLOCKED: <question>`**, 124 = timeout. **It blocks until the slice
ends**: `--detach --no-wait`, then wait on the printed `rearm` command in the background.
**Agent-tool sub-agents:** paste the output of `acp-dispatch footer --report
<scratchpad>/<name>-report.md` into the prompt verbatim (never `$(…)`); read that file, never the
final message (cut at 4,000 characters). `acp-dispatch check <dir|report>...` lists no-report and
BLOCKED slices; put them in every handoff. Why: `site-private-djbclark/memory/reference_agent_rules_ops_housekeeping.md`.

## Reading `aiuse` quota numbers

**`used_percent` is the share CONSUMED; 100 = exhausted.** Decide from `remaining_percent`; write
"100% used / 0% left", never a bare percentage. Before each batch run `aiuse --available --json
[--live]` (exit 3 = nothing usable), **always `--json`**, stderr kept out (the human table changes). A 429 names a pool (one TUI can hold several by model family), not the vendor: try its
other pool; after a real 429, `aiuse note-exhausted <provider> [--family F] --resets-in 4h53m`.
**agy: only via `acp-run agy`, never `agy -p`** (hidden burst limit; no probe loops). Detail:
`model-routing` and `bigteam` skills.

## Basic Memory — shared pool vs private pools

One shared MCP server, `http://127.0.0.1:18796/mcp`; never register a per-client stdio copy.
**Cursor cannot reach it** (no basic-memory tools despite `~/.cursor/mcp.json`): use the `bm` CLI.
**If it is down, fix it:** `launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.djbclark.basic-memory-mcp`.

1. **`main`** is the shared default: `~/ops/site-private-djbclark/memory`, git-tracked (a write is a commit);
   notes follow `site-private-djbclark/memory/schemas/`.
2. **`cursor-memory`** (`~/agent-memory/cursor`, outside git) is Cursor's private pool: name it
   explicitly, never the default; no `search_all_projects: true`.

**A semantic search result is not a match**: answer "does X exist?" with `find` or an exact
`read_note`. Detail: `site-private-djbclark/memory/basic-memory/README.md`.

## Where work happens — claimed task workspaces; `~/ops` plain `master` for now

**All development (edits, commits, tests, one-line docs) happens in an exclusively claimed task workspace (a linked worktree
or a cow pasture), one writer at a time, never in a shared checkout, which is only fast-forwarded**; before dispatching into a
workspace, `git status` and `git log -3`, stop on an unresolved merge. Exceptions: read-only inspection, a promoter's
`--ff-only`, installs on the real target (`~/src/djbclark-ade/AGENTS.md` order 9;
`memory/feedback_all_work_in_claimed_workspaces.md`). **Transitional, until the ops-promotion pilot passes:** edit
`~/ops/{stayturgid,site-public-djbclark,site-private-djbclark}` in place on `master`. Everywhere, **commit and push your own changes as each
unit lands, unasked**; default `git-commit-own MSG <paths>`, never a bare `git commit` (it takes what another agent
staged too; its pre-commit stashes every unstaged change in the tree for the hook run and clobbers parallel agents). In `~/ops`, a file also holding another agent's edits: stage only your hunks (`git apply --cached`), then
`git-commit-own --staged MSG` (a pathspec commits the working tree, their hunk included). **Running deployments read
`~/ops`**: check what reads a file first, keep commits small, prefer a quick revert.

**Nothing is version-pinned**: all software tracks its latest release; the nightly
`brew-fast-upgrade` keeps brew, npm and uv current. **Changed a third-party project's source** (a fork,
a vendored copy, a local patch)? In the same session: search its issues and PRs for a duplicate, tidy the
change, consult a `frontier` agent if it helps, then open the issue/PR upstream, so upgrades need no merges
(`memory/feedback_upstream_local_changes.md`).

**Data, committed in place:** `site-private-djbclark/memory/`, `site-public-djbclark/research/` (public). `git pull
--rebase` before editing, append to `MEMORY.md` (never rewrite it), commit exact paths, push; never
`commit -a` or `pull --autostash`. Never hand-edit the Codex summaries
(`memory/codex/memory_summary.md`, `raw_memories.md`): drop notes in `memory/codex/extensions/ad_hoc/`.
**Another agent's unstaged edits:** never stash, add or reset them; `git fetch`; only ahead: plain `git push`; behind: wait or ask.

**A stopped or `launchctl`-disabled service may be deliberate**: check
`site-private-djbclark/memory/` before "repairing" it; never clear the override (`launchctl load
-w`/`bootstrap` does); fix any role that says otherwise rather than re-enabling the unit.

## `~/s` is a view — reference `~/src`, never `~/s`

**Every path an agent writes, runs, records or hands on is `~/src/<name>` or your claimed workspace, never `~/s/...`** (a
generated, locked symlink farm); never write to or start a session in `~/s`. After adding or removing
a top-level `~/src` entry run `just -f ~/s/justfile` (`site-public-djbclark/tools/s-farm/AGENTS.md`).

## Ask where a new thing goes

**Before creating a new tool, script, skill or doc in an ops repo, ask djbclark which repo it belongs
in**, naming anything private. `site-private-djbclark` is private-only (hook-enforced; no `--no-verify`); the
rest is public `site-public-djbclark`. **Exception, no question: a new agent skill goes in
`~/src/djbclark-ade/skills/<name>/`** (link it with
`~/src/djbclark-ade/bin/skill-everywhere <name>`); only a private skill (`1password`) goes in
`site-private-djbclark/skills/`. (`memory/feedback_ask_where_new_things_go.md`)

