## NixOS rebuilds and CI

**NixOS: deploy only the pushed branch head, so every agent's committed change ships.** Push first;
`get` deploys `main` (`scripts/deploy`), vps-primary `master` promoted to `deploy-vps-primary`; never
a local, side-branch or older commit. A deploy in flight: wait, redeploy the new head, check the
deployed revision. Heavy builds and `nix flake update` run on Forgejo CI, not on vps-primary.

**Heavy jobs run in CI, not on the Mac: GitHub runners for GitHub-sourced repos (`ci/base` + `ci/run/**`,
`scripts/github-*` wrappers); Forgejo/Hyades CI for MIT/SIPB and private work that needs it** (`forgejo-ci`
skill, item 6). **Builds go to CI by repo visibility (2026-11-01): public repos on GitHub, Linux or macOS for
macOS-only builds; private Linux on Forgejo/Hyades; private macOS-only builds stay local via `bg`. No macOS leg in
PR checks unless djbclark asks for it by name** (`memory/feedback_macos_ci_on_request_only.md`).

## Reply formatting — number every list

**Every list in a reply to djbclark is labelled, never plain `-`/`*` bullets** (tables are fine). Each
top-level heading gets a letter (`A. Why it failed`, `B. Still open`); its items are `A1`, `A2`, …,
sub-items `A1a`, so every heading and item has a short, unique designation djbclark can type.
A reply with no headings uses `1.`, `2.`; question-card options are numbered too. Label each
item once: never a `1.` list marker plus an `A1` label. Every URL is a markdown link
`[label](url)`; anything to click, copy or paste goes in prose before a question card, which Paseo
renders unselectable and unlinkified (getpaseo/paseo#3164).

## Other agent CLIs, and delegating to them

Binary names differ from provider ids (antigravity → `agy`, zai → `zcode`, opencode-go → `opencode`,
cursor → `cursor-agent`; table: `memory/reference_agent_rules_code_discovery_and_cli_table.md`);
gemini is deprecated, not installed. Every agent but muse speaks ACP (`acp-run --list`).

**Delegation route, in order:** (1) **`acp-run <agent> -C <dir> -p '<prompt>' --model <m>`**
(`~/.local/bin/acp-run`; **always `--model`**, zcode too; `--effort`/`--context`
too; check its `applied:` line; yolo-only, `--perm deny` for review-only slices; exit
0 is not success, verify tests/diff); (2) `paseo run`; (3) non-ACP CLIs:
their own headless recipe. herdr (bar herdr4paseo/paseo4herdr) and Orca are retired; GUI control
is `paseo-screen`. **copilot is the only reserve: floor 60% left, one small GitHub slice**;
grok is an ordinary pool (`acp-run grok --model grok-4.7`). **hermes is never a delegation target. Never an all-vendor
fan-out**: `route_agent.py plan` picks vendor × model × effort per slice. **cline (uninstalled)
never without djbclark's explicit approval for that run** (refused unless
`ACP_OPERATOR_APPROVED=cline`). **The Claude API credits (`omp`) are a normal tier,
used with discernment: `api-only` work first, else code/bulk slices via `acp-run omp --model anthropic/claude-...`,
never left unspent** (`djbclark-ade/skills/model-routing/SKILL.md`).

**Routing policy: minimise cost, maximise code quality.** Frontier models are scarce:
judgment is Opus `high`; codex `gpt-6-astra` and Fable `high` only for work that really needs them
(codex one slice in flight, Fable `xhigh` almost never); codex on other models may also take non-frontier
work when its quota is plentiful and the router ranks it the best available (
`memory/feedback_routing_policy_cost_quality.md`); everyday work on other pools at the minimum
spec. **claude codes on Sonnet 5.5, never Fable**; quota never buys a pool more than 10 rank steps
below the best into code/judgment. Claude tiers: Haiku lookups, Sonnet executes, Opus plans, reviews
and advises, Fable frontier; climb on evidence. **`acp-dispatch` needs `--kind`, refuses an ineligible
pool, and runs `frontier` as an advisor unless `ACP_OPERATOR_APPROVED=frontier-exec`; cheapest eligible
tier first.** One copy: `skills/model-routing/SKILL.md` *Routing policy* (numbers:
`djbclark-ade/config/routing.toml`).

## Multi-agent toolkit — know these exist

**`~/src/djbclark-ade` is the agent toolchain: use it before hand-rolling anything, and fix it there**:
skills, `acp-dispatch`/`acp-run`/`route_agent.py`, `docs/orchestration-practices.md`.
**Every** skill lives there, linked into each TUI by `skill-everywhere` (edit the
git copy, never a TUI copy; list: `docs/skills.md`), e.g. `bigteam`, `model-routing`,
`session-finder` (every "tell the agent doing X" relay), `helm`, `herdr-tidy`, `autorename`.

## Context size: delegate early, compact rarely

**Keep context small: by default send discrete, separable tasks to `bigteam`** (or a sub-agent) at the
minimum model × effort × context that does it well; past ~40k tokens of growth, delegate from then
on. Compaction is **automatic, never a question to djbclark**; in Paseo
compact earlier, once, at a natural boundary: `~/src/djbclark-ade/bin/self-slash "/compact
<focus>"`; a topic change runs the `handoff` skill then `/new`. self-slash refuses while anything is
on the input line; only then ask djbclark to type it, never send over a draft. Never touch another
bigteam session's claimed files or panes (herdr tab `coord`).

**Compact Instructions**: when compacting or summarising, keep the current task and its
status; files touched (absolute paths); djbclark's rulings and decisions this session; open items and
the exact next step; handoff, report and scratchpad paths. Omit tool output, dead ends and anything
already in a committed file.

## Fix the cause of a tool-calling mistake

**A tool-calling mistake of a kind that can recur: fix the instructions that would have prevented it
in the same turn, unasked**, and say what you changed
(`memory/feedback_fix_instructions_after_tool_mistake.md`). **An agent-harness failure gets a
root-cause fix in `~/src/djbclark-ade`, code not just docs**; re-routing is not the fix (
`memory/feedback_fix_harness_failures_in_djbclark_ade.md`).

## Ping djbclark on Hermes

**When a task completes or something needs djbclark**, also run `~/.local/bin/hermes-ping "<repo>: <one
line>"`, never bare `hermes send`: one line per event, never a loop; every PR, issue or page as a full
URL, never `repo#N`; script notices too, not macOS notifications.
(`memory/feedback_ping_telegram_and_chat.md`)

## Periodic updates are a script, not a cron prompt

**Asked for periodic updates ("keep me posted")? Use a script, never a recurring model prompt**
(CronCreate, `/loop`): each firing is a full model turn that re-reads the whole conversation and usually
finds nothing new. Use or extend `~/ops/site-private-djbclark/bin/fleet-watch` (no tokens, notifies on change) or
write a similar script. **Allowed:** a one-shot future check, and a recurring wake of an agent that no
script or event can wake, when every firing has real work to do.
(`memory/feedback_periodic_updates_by_script.md`)

## Heavy builds and tests: run them through `bg`

**Run builds and tests (pytest, tox, nox, `run_tests*.py`) via `~/ops/site-private-djbclark/bin/bg`** (**always
the full path, bare `bg` is the shell builtin**), in the background. **CI first**: `bg`
refuses (exit 3) a repo with no `load-watch ci decide DIR ci|local` decision. It never starts
"anyway": it waits up to 6 h for load and a slot, then exits 75, and pings djbclark when a run goes
long. **Never set `BG_LOAD_WAIT=0` on a build or Gradle command** (one small test file only). Changed
files first (`--lf -x`), the full suite only when asked. **Never `taskpolicy -b` for builds**; never
throttle or SIGSTOP another session's processes.

**Gradle: one build at a time, machine-wide.** Check `pgrep -fl
'GradleWrapperMain|gradle-wrapper.jar'` first; "Waiting for the machine-wide Gradle slot" means
queued, not hung: don't kill or retry it. Profiles: default `low`; `night` only with an expiry
(`bin/gradle-limits set night --until 07:00`); don't override with `--max-workers` or `-Dorg.gradle.*`. Detail: site-public-djbclark `docs/gradle-limits.md`.

**Claim a dev-server port in `site-public-djbclark/registry/ports.yml` before binding it; drive test browsers headless** (`memory/feedback_claim_ports_headless_browser.md`).

## Waiting on background work, and reading results

Start slow work (over ~10-15 s) in the background; an Agent-tool sub-agent waits in bounded foreground
calls. Wait on a file or a PID (`while kill -0 pid`); **never `pgrep -f`/`pkill -f
'<pattern>'`**: it matches your own shell. **Never read a result or exit status through
a pipe**: `cmd > out.log 2>&1; echo "rc=$?" > out.rc; tail -5 out.log`, then read `out.rc`, never the
notification. **Never credit a hook you have not seen run.** Never feed a `--body-file`
from a pipeline: write the file, check `[ -s f ]` first. (`memory/feedback_exit_status_through_pipe.md`)

## Code discovery — symbol tools before `cat`/`rg`

**`graft` is the navigator in every repo** (`graft build` first if it has no `graft/`): the `graft` CLI
before `cat`/`rg`/Grep for **code discovery**; plain text search is right for prose and config. Relay
graft's "tokens saved ≈ N" banner as a total. `rtk` owns Bash output compaction: **never run `ts init`**. A no-hit `graft grep`: retry
with the bare symbol or a short literal. Defects: `site-public-djbclark/docs/tooling-policy.md`.

## Run commands yourself

Run every command yourself, including Touch ID / `sudo-ask` steps and interactive confirmations; never
tell djbclark to type a `!` command. Privileged steps still use their sanctioned path.

## Tools and habits

1. **Fast tools:** `rg` **with a path, never `-r` or bare `grep -r`** (`rtk grep` is BSD grep;
   `bash_habits_gate.py` denies recursive `grep`/`ggrep`), `fd`, `ast-grep`, `uv` not `pip`,
   **`aria2c -x8 -s8` for big files**, **`dust`/`gdu` not `du`/`find` for cache sizing**.
   **Literal replace: `srgn -L --fail-none --stdin-detection force-unreadable --glob RELPATH
   'FIND' -- 'REPL'` from the repo root, never `~`**; exit 1 also means nothing matched or nothing in scope
   (absolute glob, gitignored or dot-directory file, FIND starting `-`: use the Edit tool); **REPL with `\`
   goes through Python with an assert**; **`sd` is retired for edits**. **Mac code uses brew GNU `g*` tools
   where BSD differs** (`gnu-tools-check`); **always `gsed`, never bare `sed`** (
   `bash_habits_gate.py` denies it as a Cursor `beforeShellExecution` hook too). **Root/NOPASSWD scripts keep
   `/usr/bin` tools**; **test a NOPASSWD rule with `sudo -k -n CMD`**, never plain `sudo -n`.
   (`memory/feedback_modern_cli_tools.md`) **Building a command string, quoting, a loop, a conditional or
   parsing output? Write Python with an argv list (`subprocess.run([...])`), never a nested shell string**: a
   heredoc inside `ssh "..."` truncates silently and exits 0. One plumbing command stays shell;
   `rg`/`fd`/`jq` stay the command-line tools. (`memory/feedback_prefer_python_over_shell.md`) **Find files:
   Spotlight is off on purpose (`mdfind` finds nothing). A file over a day old: `plocate -i NAME`
   (`/opt/homebrew/var/lib/plocate/plocate.db`, nightly 03:30) or NeoFinder; newer: a scoped `fd`/`find` under
   1-2 likely dirs, with `timeout`; else ask djbclark. Never `fd`/`find` over all of `~`**.
   (`memory/feedback_find_files_plocate_neofinder_not_spotlight_or_home_scan.md`)
2. **Replace shared scripts atomically** (temp file in the same directory, then `mv -f`) **on the `realpath`**, never in
   place. (`memory/feedback_edit_running_scripts_atomically.md`)
3. **Default browser:** `open <url>` only, never Orion or Chrome by name. (`memory/feedback_always_use_default_browser_orion.md`)
4. **"All agents" = every session `~/src/djbclark-ade/skills/session-finder/fleet.py --json` lists with a usable channel**: Paseo, terminal Claude, live ACP launch, Hermes (which `aiuse` doesn't list); not finished ACP launches (relay refuses them) or channel-less `pid*` rows. **An idle agent gets a plain relay prompt, never `--steer`** (steered text to an idle agent is queued and never read); steer only a working one. A quota-guard line (e.g. "critical") binds only the pool it names (Claude Code); omp, codex, grok, agy, zai, opencode keep working. (`memory/feedback_all_agents_includes_hermes.md`)
5. **New tool, MCP server or plugin? Ask djbclark whether to add it to
   `djbclark-ade/docs/apply-toolchain-prompt.md`; a new skill goes in unasked unless sensitive.**
   (`memory/feedback_ask_update_apply_toolchain_prompt.md`) **AI TUIs, agent CLIs and ACP servers stay on the latest release:
   upgrade unasked, never hard-code a version path.** (`memory/feedback_ai_tuis_always_latest.md`)
6. **Never pass `--help` to a live-service CLI subcommand**; `collie` is read-only for agents. **Secrets: `sudo-secretspec`
   only; load its skill before scripting it** (every subcommand needs `--reason`). **App configs (codexbar, goose, hermes) hold plaintext keys: print key names or booleans, never raw lines or ranges**. (`memory/feedback_no_help_flag_on_live_service_cli.md`, `memory/feedback_always_use_secretspec.md`)
7. **Web search and fetch walk the failover ladder** (`fallback-chains` skill, `web-get`): **search first with the shared
   `firecrawl` MCP's `firecrawl_search`** (`mcp__firecrawl__*`); **never the paid cloud `mcp__claude_ai_Firecrawl__*`** unless
   djbclark asks. **Every page the task needs is eventually fetched**: fall back, fix the tool, or pause and fix it with
   djbclark; never accept "unfetchable", continue without the source, or fill it in from memory. (`memory/feedback_web_failover_pause.md`)

## Clipboard — never bare `pbcopy`

**"copy to clipboard"/"pbcopy" means `~/ops/site-private-djbclark/bin/clip`**, never bare `pbcopy` (agent shells
have `LC_CTYPE=C`); human-bound prose gets `clip --unwrap`, no Markdown. **Every prompt you write for djbclark to
hand to another agent also goes through `clip`, unasked**. Save and restore the pasteboard around tests.

## Opening or revealing local files — only on request

**Open or reveal local files or folders only on djbclark’s direct request or explicit yes.** Otherwise provide a
clickable path. Report authorized openings. (`memory/feedback_reveal_files_in_finder.md`)

## Long documents — query the book KB, never paste the book

Books: `~/ops/site-private-djbclark/bin/book-kb`, cheapest first (`book-kb query '<regex>' <slug>` is exact,
then `toc`, then one chapter); a semantic hit is not a match. **Never read `~/kb/raw/<slug>.md`.**
Query `learning-cfengine` before writing or reviewing CFEngine policy. Detail:
`site-private-djbclark/docs/book-kb-vs-book-to-skill.md`.

## Never waste spent tokens without asking

**Don't throw away work already paid for without asking djbclark first**: no stopping a running agent
partway, restarting from scratch, or rerunning something whose result exists. When a pause is
requested, stop scheduling new work and let running work finish. **Breakage found by accident: always
ask djbclark whether to fix it, last in the queue**.

## Agents run in yolo (auto-approve) mode by default

**Every agent, every TUI, however launched, runs in its yolo-or-equivalent mode**; `--perm deny` only
for a security-sensitive slice (prompt-injection exposure; cursor cannot deny at all,
acp-run refuses it). A read-only review runs yolo with a read-only brief and a diff check. Yolo never skips hooks, file-ownership rules, reserve pools, or asking before hard-to-reverse or
outward-facing actions. Detail: `site-private-djbclark/memory/feedback_yolo_default_for_all_agents.md`.

## Session logs (Tier 1 handoff pointers)

At session start in a git repo, read its Tier 1 pointer (`~/.local/state/handoffs/`), compare
